# ---------------- Edges of the map, drowned lands and things nobody explains ----------------
@who Explorer
@levels *
every map of albion ends in water, [but|and] the water doesn't end. [swim long enough|keep swimming] and [the world stops you|you hit the edge]
lyonesse was a drowned land. [on a still night|when it's quiet] [you can hear|some hear] [bells under the water|church bells under the sea]
i keep a [map|list] of every cave in albion nobody [talks about|names]. [it's longer than you'd think|it keeps growing]
the coast of cornwall hides caves you can only reach from the water. found two so far
the pixie queen in campacorentin holds court somewhere off the paths. i followed the giggling once
granddaddy longlegs is a real name in campacorentin and i refuse to explain further
there's a white horse walking salisbury. [old folks say|i heard] it's [the one carved into the hills|older than the stones]
cynewulf the ghostwalker is not a briton name. someone else's war ended in salisbury
they found inishail just off the coast and you can swim there. makes you wonder how many more islands are hiding out there
the spirit of lord emthoro still holds inishail. nobody knows who he was
anyone else ever swum out past the last island just to see what happens?

@who *
@levels 25-50
the vetusta abbey bell rings in the black mountains, they say. i wonder who it rings for
cailleach uragaig walks lyonesse. the old folk in cornwall won't say the name
there's a thing called draco magnificens in the pennines, they say. never seen it
coliath rules gwyddneau and nobody can tell me what it guards
hadrian's wall was built to keep something out. nobody agrees what
the eye of arawn in snowdonia watches the whole frontier, they say. i wave when i pass

@who Socialite
@levels *
the bards in camelot can't agree whether lyonesse sank in one night or over a hundred years. i side with one night

# ======================= Debate scenes: Albion lore =======================
@kind debate
@realm alb

@topic lore_avalon
@levels 1-50
@scene alb_lore_avalon_in_the_marsh
@role B type=Explorer
@role C type=Leveler,NoLifer levels=30-50
@role D type=* levels=5-25
@role E type=Socialite,Daredevil
A: {q} anyone else think avalon was hidden in the marsh all along? | {q} i get more sure every week: avalon was in the marsh the whole time
?D: wait, avalon like the avalonians? i thought that was just a race on the character screen | avalon as in avalonians? i figured that was just a race name
B: thats the thing. day one the race text said they come from the city of avalon, albion's seat of magic. so where was it? | the race text says avalonians come from the city of avalon, where the great mage guilds were. ever been? nobody had
C: across the water, where we found it. avalon isle, the isle of glass, the ruins. case closed | on the isle, where it is now. we sailed there, we killed things there. done
B: the isle we found was rubble. the drakoran had already burned it. so who was the race text talking about? | by the time we landed it was ash and drakoran. so where did the living avalonians come from?
C: survivors. thats why they came to camelot. thats literally the race story | refugees. they fled to camelot, which is why they have nowhere to go home to
B: then why did the old guides say avalon marsh runs right to the edges of avalon city. edges. you dont have edges across a sea | the first guides said the marsh ran to the edges of avalon city. a city on another island has no edges in our marsh
E: the old guides also said the merchants were honest | the old guides also said my class was fun to solo
B: walk the marsh sometime. roman pillars sinking into the bog, in rows. rows lead somewhere | go stand in the marsh. pillars, half sunk, in lines. somebody built a road into that mud
C: romans built everywhere. there's a whole roman tomb under cornwall and nobody calls cardova avalon | the romans left stones in every zone. cardova has an emperor in it and it isnt avalon either
D: whats in the marsh besides pillars? i only go there for the slimes | is there anything in the marsh worth seeing? i just kill slimes there
B: lady nimue. the lady of the lake. she walks the marsh after dark, every night, and nobody can tell you why | the lady of the lake walks that marsh at night. not a guard, not a merchant. her
C: she walks camelot hills and campacorentin too. she just likes long walks | she has a whole route, four zones. by your logic avalon is everywhere
B: think about the old tale. the lady took excalibur back, and arthur was carried to avalon to heal. she keeps the way in | in the old story the lady of the lake took the sword back and the barge took arthur to avalon. she is the gatekeeper
E: so arthur is asleep under the marsh? would explain why camelot went downhill | arthur's napping in a bog? explains a lot about the realm lately
B: the old tale says he sleeps in avalon until albion needs him most. look around. albion needs him | the isle of apples, where the king sleeps until the realm is at its worst. we're getting close
C: if he's sleeping in that marsh he picked a smelly bed | he'd wake up just to complain about the leeches
D: wait so what's the isle of glass then, if avalon is somewhere else? | then why is the landing called the isle of glass?
B: isle of glass is an old name for glastonbury, and glastonbury was always said to be avalon. look at the names over there | glastonbury was the isle of glass, and the monks swore it was avalon. the isle is wearing avalon's old names
C: so you agree. avalon is the isle. across the water. not under your frogs | caer witrin, wearyall, all on the isle. you just proved my point
B: i agree the isle wears the names. i think the real city sat between the two, and the mist moved it | the names went with the people who fled. the city itself? the mist has it
~ 15
C: mist doesnt move cities | fog does not pick up a city and carry it off
B: mist pulled a whole pass out of a bog in hibernia once, ask any hib. their rangers swore it wasnt there the week before | the hibs had a pass come out of the mist one morning. nobody believed that either
E: great, now we're taking geography lessons from hibs | lovely, hib fairy stories as evidence
A: ok explorer, what's your actual proof | so whats the proof, if there is any
B: none i can hand you. but the fog west of adribard's retreat doesnt move with the wind. real fog moves | the fog off adribard's retreat sits still in a gale. ive watched it for an hour. fog that doesnt drift is a door
C: i'll believe it when the fog lets you in and you send me a tell from inside | send me a tell from the other side and i'll buy you a horse
?E: bring a lantern. and a healer. and dont call me when the leeches win | count me out, i'll hold your corpse spot
A: i ride past the marsh after this camp anyway. i'll look at the fog | im near the marsh later, i'll check the fog for you
B: look at the pillars too. count which way they lean | and look which way the pillars lean. they all point the same way

@topic lore_avalon
@levels 1-50
@scene alb_lore_avalon_why_a_ship
@role B type=NoLifer levels=30-50
@role C type=Explorer
@role D type=* levels=5-25
@role E type=Socialite,Daredevil
A: {q} if avalon sat inside albion all along, why did we have to cross the sea to reach it | honest question for the marsh crowd: if avalon was always ours, why did it take a crossing to get there
?D: wait, there's an actual avalon you can go to? i thought the avalonians were just a race on the creation screen | hold on, avalon is a real place? i thought it was flavour text for the avalonian race
B: oh it's real. the isles opened it. you land at caer gothwaite on the isle of glass and walk straight into drakoran country | real enough to kill you. the shrouded isles came out, you land at caer gothwaite and the drakoran are waiting
B: and i say that as an avalonian since the first week. a homeland on my creation screen and no road home for a year | i rolled an avalonian at launch. a city of mages in my story and not one road on the map that led there
C: because the old avalon was never a place you walk to. in the old tale arthur went there on a barge, three queens with him. by water | the old story never had a road either. arthur was carried there on a barge with three queens. water was always the way in
E: three queens on one barge. meanwhile i cant get one cleric to follow me to the next camp | three queens to row one king. my group cant even find one healer
C: and the real glastonbury, the isle of glass, was a hill standing up out of drowned marsh. the marsh and the island are one story | look at old glastonbury. a hill sticking out of flooded marsh, and they called it an island. marsh and isle, same place
B: then why is our marsh on the mainland and the isle a sea crossing away? thats not a hill in a bog, thats open water | nice theory, but our marsh is on one side of the map and the isle is over open sea. no bog in between
C: because the land moved, or the mist did. hibernia had a whole pass come out of a bog. an island can drift off a marsh | if a pass can come out of the mist in hibernia, an island can slip back into it here
B: that hib mist pass gets used to prove everything from avalon to free respecs | every albion argument ends up borrowing that hib pass story
D: so is the city still there? the one the avalonians came from? | so what happened to the city of mages, is any of it left?
B: avalon city is a dungeon now. rubble and drakoran, named ones like dura'ek the empowered and sys'sro the ruthless giving orders | its a dungeon. drakoran sitting in the old libraries, dura'ek the empowered and sys'sro the ruthless in charge
E: sys'sro the ruthless. you just know his mother calls him sysy | sys'sro the ruthless sounds like a name somebody practised in a mirror
C: and under it the crystal cave with xanxicar. a city of mages built on a two-headed dragon. tell me that was an accident | the crystal cave sits right under the ruins, xanxicar and all. nobody builds a mage city on a dragon by mistake
~ 15
B: what bothers me is the word when the isles were announced: avalon was no longer a friendly place. no longer. so it was friendly once | the first word about the isles said avalon was no longer a friendly place. no longer. it had a friendly time we never got to see
C: exactly. it was there and friendly the whole time we were levelling in the marsh. hidden, not missing | right, friendly and there, behind the mist, while we killed slimes next door
D: is anyone on our side still living over there? | who still lives on the isle then?
B: wearyall holds out, a hamlet of avalonians. in the old tale wearyall hill is where joseph's staff took root and grew the holy thorn | wearyall village. the name is from the legend too: wearyall hill, where a pilgrim's staff grew into a thorn tree
E: a village named after a tired hill. i feel that every time i ride from caer witrin to camelot | wearyall. clearly named by someone who walked there
C: and the orchards. crafted apple gatherers, made things picking fruit for nobody. avalon means isle of apples in the old tongue | the isle of apples, avalons oldest name. and who works the orchards now? crafted gatherers, built not born
B: ok, the apples i give you. the first time i saw one picking fruit it gave me a chill | the apple harvesters got me, i admit it
A: so it was always there, it was hidden, and the drakoran found the door before we did | so the city was there, friendly, hidden, and then the drakoran knocked first
B: and that's the real question. someone showed them the way. dragon-men dont read old maps | the question isnt the ship, its who led the drakoran across first
?E: probably whoever sold me a map of the marsh in camelot for forty silver | someone with a boat and no morals. half the merchants in camelot qualify
C: next trip i'm following the orchard down to the shore. if an old road ran out to the marsh, it ends in the water there | i'll walk the orchard edge to the coast next time. if there was ever a road home, it starts there
B: send a tell if you find it. i've only been waiting since launch | if you find a road home, tell this avalonian first

@topic lore_avalon
@levels 1-50
@scene alb_lore_xanxicar_the_lock
@role B type=NoLifer,Leveler levels=35-50
@role C type=Explorer
@role D type=* levels=5-25
@role E class=Wizard,Theurgist,Sorcerer,Cabalist
A: {q} here's a thought i cant shake. xanxicar was under avalon before the first mage laid a stone | which came first, the city of avalon or the two-headed dragon in its cellar
?D: xanxicar? is that one of the dragons you raid? | wait, who is xanxicar, another dragon?
B: twin-headed dragon in the crystal cave, under the ruins of avalon city. one of the big beasts of the isles, raid only | two heads, one dragon, lives in the crystal cave under the ruined city. raid only, dont go look
B: and the cave fills with lightning if you take too long. thats the part everyone remembers | linger and the whole lair lights up with lightning. thats how most raids there end
C: think about that. a dragon whose cave punishes you for staying. thats not a lair, thats a guarded door | a beast whose home turns on you if you overstay. thats not a temper, thats a ward on a door
B: or a short fuse. they even lengthened the lightning timer in a patch. nobody makes a sacred ward easier | the timer got longer in a patch because raids kept wiping. thats tuning, not a sacred seal
E: speaking as a wizard: lightning that builds on a timer is how a ward feels. it stores, then it spends. a storm doesnt keep time | lightning that gathers and then breaks on a count is how a binding behaves. weather has no clock
B: a wizard says it's magic. stop the presses | of course the wizard thinks everything is a spell
E: the race text calls that city the seat of albion's strongest mage guilds. mages notice what sleeps under their floors | the greatest mage guilds in albion lived up there. you think they missed a dragon downstairs?
C: there's an old tale for it. vortigern's walls kept falling down until the boy merlin told him: two dragons sleep in a pool under the hill | remember the old story. vortigern's fort kept collapsing and young merlin said dig: a red dragon and a white one under the foundations
D: two dragons under a castle? like two heads? | wait, two dragons under the hill, and ours has two heads?
C: two heads, one body. the old tale made it two dragons. avalon kept them together under the city | the legend split them in two. here they share one body under the mage city
B: in that tale they were dug up and fought, and the red one won. it was a prophecy, not a door lock | they got dug out and the red beat the white. nobody kept them as a lock, it was an omen
C: an omen about the fate of the land, buried under walls on purpose. call it what you like, someone kept it there | the fate of the realm buried under a fort. thats still a lock with a story carved on it
~ 15
B: then the lock failed. drakoran, dragon-men, walked into the city and burned the libraries | some lock. the drakoran strolled in and burned the place
C: thats my best evidence. dragon-men walking past a dragon guard. maybe the guard let its own kin through | dragon-men, dragon guard. who do you think opened the door
E: or flip it. the guilds were the ward and xanxicar the prisoner. burn the guilds and the prisoner stirs | maybe the mages were the lock and the dragon was the thing locked in. kill the mages, the ward weakens
B: then why is he still sitting in his cave instead of flying over camelot | then why hasnt he left. he could be eating cotswold by now
E: a ward half broken still holds the door. maybe the lightning is what's left of the old spell | broken wards still bite. the lightning could be the last of it, working on its own
?D: so if you kill him, does something get out? | so killing him might let something loose?
B: raids have dropped him and the only thing that came out of the crystal cave was the raid, carrying loot | every raid that downs him gets loot and a long ride home. nothing has crawled out yet
C: yet. the drakoran never left those ruins. ask yourself what they're still digging for | that we know of. the drakoran are still in the city, still searching for something
A: this is better than another argument about drop tables honestly | i like this more than the loot debates
B: fine. next time a raid goes down there i'll watch the drakoran when he falls. if they cheer, i owe you a drink | next raid i'll keep an eye on the drakoran when he drops. if they look relieved, you win
C: and count the seconds to the lightning. a storm doesnt count. a ward does | time the lightning while you're at it. if it keeps a clock, i'm right

@topic lore_dragon
@levels 1-50
@scene alb_lore_golestandt_he_or_she
@role B type=NoLifer levels=30-50
@role C type=Explorer,Socialite
@role D type=* levels=5-25
@role E class=Minstrel
A: {q} settle a guild fight for me. golestandt, he or she | my guild has argued for an hour. is golestandt a he or a she
?D: who's golestandt? | golestandt? is that a boss?
B: albion's dragon. the stone dragon at the south edge of dartmoor, past the granite giants. and she. every herald writes she | our dragon. south end of dartmoor, a dragon like living rock. and it's she, the heralds always wrote she
C: every raid leader i ever followed said he. "he's turning, he's breathing, spread out" | funny, the raid leaders i rode with all said he. "he's lifting his head, scatter"
B: raid leaders call everything he. i heard one call the king's wight a she once and nobody blinked | a raid leader would call a door he if it hit hard enough
E: the songs say she. there's an old minstrel verse about the stone mother of dartmoor. you dont sing a mother as a he | minstrels have sung her as the stone mother for years. nobody rhymes a mother with he
C: since when do minstrels get facts right. one of you sang that i soloed a giant | minstrels also sing that the merchants in camelot are fair
D: why does it even matter? | does it change anything in the fight?
B: it matters because a name was all we had. sir arward's thirty knights rode into dartmoor and the only word that came back was hers | the name came first. sir arward led thirty knights into the moor and all that returned was a journal with one word: golestandt
E: and that journal didnt say he or she. one singed word, fallen out of the sky over prydwen keep | one word and no pronoun. the page dropped out of the sky at prydwen, half burnt
C: there you go. the only witness never said she. the heralds decided later | the one real witness never said. so "she" came from someone who wasnt there
B: the heralds wrote the dartmoor story. if anyone decides, it's them | the people who wrote her lore say she. thats as close to a witness as we get
~ 15
C: midgard cant decide about theirs either. half of them say he for the wolf-headed one and half say she | the mids argue the same thing about their wolf dragon. its a dragon thing, not an albion thing
B: and a hib once told me their glimmer king is the only male dragon of the three. king, so that one's easy | some hib swore their glimmer king is the only he among the dragons. a king. fair enough
D: what does she actually do to you? the breath? | so how does the fight go, is it the fire breath?
B: two breaths. a patch put a heat debuff on her breath so the next one hurts more, i think. a later patch softened one of them | she breathes twice and the first strips your heat resist, as i understand it. one breath got toned down later, still hurts
C: and he drops fifty luminescent stones for respecs. half the raid is there for the stones, not the pronoun | and the stones. fifty respec stones a kill. nobody asks he or she when the stones drop
E: in the old tale uther took the name pendragon when a star shaped like a dragon crossed the sky. albion has always argued about dragons | uther was called pendragon after the dragon star he saw. our kings have been arguing about dragons since the beginning
C: pendragon, chief dragon. and uther was a he, by the way | and uther was very much a he. just saying
B: uther is a shade in the barrows. she is alive in dartmoor. different argument | uther haunts the barrows. she lives on the moor. not the same file
A: still split then. i'm calling her she, in case she can hear us from the moor | ok. she, to be safe. dartmoor is closer than it looks
?E: the next verse says "the dragon". everyone pays, everyone's happy | i'll write the ballad as "the dragon" and keep both camps buying me ale

@topic lore_cardova
@levels 1-50
@scene alb_lore_cardova_nameless_emperor
@role B type=Explorer
@role C type=NoLifer levels=30-50
@role D type=* levels=5-25
@role E type=Socialite,Daredevil
A: {q} how does a crypt hold an emperor nobody can name. cardova bugs me | cardova question. the lore says a roman emperor no one can name lies there. how do you lose an emperor
?D: cardova is the place in cornwall with the roman dead, right? | is cardova the roman crypt under cornwall? i havent been yet
C: cardova, yes. legionaries, centurions, praetorians, all dead and still standing their posts | the crypts under cornwall. dead legionaries and centurions and the princeps praetorii, a legion that never got the order to stand down
B: and the story says it plainly: an emperor of rome buried in cornwall, and his name lost. not hidden. lost | the lore is simple. an emperor of rome lies in cornwall and nobody can name him. that's the whole record
C: except emperor seleucus is right there with his name over his head. a named emperor, in the crypt. solved | but emperor seleucus is down there, name and all. there's your emperor
B: and a few halls away the forgotten emperor, and the spirit of the emperor. three emperors in one crypt, one name between them | then explain the forgotten emperor. and the spirit of the emperor. three crowns and one name
E: three emperors in one basement and still not one of them can tell you where the exit is | three emperors, one tomb. sounds like the queue at the camelot smith
C: seleucus isnt even a roman name. greek kings used it. smells like a pretender who took the chair when the real one died | seleucus sounds greek, not roman. a usurper's name, or a guard's joke
B: there you go. seleucus is the name on the throne, the forgotten one is the man who earned it | so the one with the name is the pretender and the forgotten one is the true emperor
D: why would rome bury an emperor in cornwall though? isnt rome far away? | wait, why is a roman emperor buried out in cornwall at all?
C: rome held this land for centuries. hadrian's wall in the north, pillars in the marsh, centurion favius still drilling out in cornwall | the romans were everywhere here. their wall up north, their pillars in the marsh, centurion favius still marching in cornwall
B: and rome had a custom for hated emperors. they chiselled the name off every stone and coin. the old books call it condemnation of memory | rome used to scrape a hated emperor's name off every monument. maybe it was done to him, on purpose
C: then he was punished, not mysterious. some failed emperor dumped at the edge of the world | so he wasnt lost, he was erased. a bad emperor thrown in a provincial hole
B: and his legion still guards him. men dont guard a hated emperor for a thousand years | hated by rome, maybe. not by his men. they're still at their posts
~ 15
C: the dead guard crypts because thats what dead things do in albion. the barrows have guards, mithra has guards | skeletons standing guard is the most normal thing in albion. every barrow has them
B: the barrow wights are a rabble. cardova keeps rank. amminus pilus, taras princep praetorii, crotus, olivarius. that's a court | look at the names down there. pilus, princeps, praetorii, all in order. thats a court still serving
D: is the forgotten emperor hard? | can a group my level take the forgotten emperor?
C: he's no dragon. it's the praetorians in the tight halls that wipe groups, and the pulls on the stairs | not hard as emperors go. the guards in the corridors are what kill you
E: bring a cleric who likes stairs. cardova is all stairs and regret | bring a healer and a sense of direction. cardova eats both
B: the old belief is that a soul whose name is forgotten cant rest. nobody says his name, so he walks | they say a soul nobody can name can never sleep. so the forgotten one stays up
E: by that logic every alt i ever deleted is haunting something | then my forgotten alts should be haunting somebody too
A: so the dead know and they wont tell. typical cornwall | nobody knows, the dead wont say, cornwall shrugs. sounds right
B: next run i'm reading the walls instead of the loot. if a name was scraped off a stone, the scrape is still there | i'm going down with a scout to look at the stonework. a chiselled-off name leaves marks
?C: if you find it, keep it quiet. the last thing we need is a fourth emperor | find a name and some bard will have a fourth emperor by friday

@topic lore_merlin
@levels 1-50
@scene alb_lore_merlin_in_the_barrow
@role B type=NoLifer,Leveler levels=25-50
@role C type=Explorer
@role D type=* levels=5-25
@role E class=Wizard,Theurgist,Sorcerer,Cabalist
A: {q} merlin is not dead. i will die on this hill | does anyone still think merlin is lying in his barrow under stonehenge
?D: merlin has a grave in this game? where? | wait, merlin is buried somewhere we can go?
B: the stonehenge barrows, under salisbury plains. the heart of it is the barrow of merlin. his name on his tomb. dead | stonehenge barrows in salisbury. the middle of it is called the barrow of merlin. nobody names a tomb after a living man
C: and who do you meet down there? the shade of uther pendragon, the king's wight, wizard lichas. never once merlin | the barrows hold uther's shade, the king's wight and wizard lichas. i've walked every hall. no merlin
B: shades are what's left when the body rots. maybe there's nothing of him left to walk | maybe he's dust. not every great man comes back as a shade
C: read the realm's own story. after arthur fell, merlin was seen no more. thats how you write a man who left, not one who died | the realm lore says merlin is seen no more. not dead, not buried. seen no more
E: and in the old tale he didnt die either. nimue learned his own spells and sealed him away, in a tree or a cave or a prison of air | the old tale has the lady of the lake learning his magic and shutting him in. a tree, a cave, a cell of air. alive, just stuck
D: nimue? like the lady who wanders around the marsh? | is that the same lady nimue who walks around at night?
C: the same. she walks camelot hills, campacorentin, the marsh and snowdonia. snowdonia, where castle myrddin keeps his staff | the very same. and her path runs through snowdonia, right past castle myrddin where merlin's staff sits as our relic
B: she hands wizards their quests on a clock. if she's guarding merlin she's very relaxed about it | she's a quest giver on a schedule, not a jailer on patrol
E: i've done her lake quests. she talks like someone who has kept a secret for a very long time | her quests are all about the lake and the old magic. she knows more than an errand giver should
B: then who named the barrow? someone buried him there, or believed they did | then why is the barrow his. somebody thought he was in it
C: in the old story merlin brought those stones over the sea himself to mark the graves of kings. his barrow because he built it | the old tale says merlin carried the stones from across the water for a royal grave. he made the barrow, he didnt fill it
E: and the old chronicles bury uther inside that ring of stones. look who walks the barrows: uther's shade. that part they got right | the chronicles put uther's grave in the stone ring, and the shade of uther pendragon is down there. the old books were half right
~ 15
B: fine, but wizard lichas. a wizard, in merlin's barrow, turned lich. i dont like where that points | and lichas? a wizard gone lich in the barrow of merlin. you see where i'm going
C: merlin would sooner be a toad than a lich. he had style | lichas is no merlin. lichas has a robe full of moths
D: whats a lich anyway? | is a lich like a skeleton mage?
E: a caster who cheated death by keeping his life outside his body. exactly the kind of trick an old wizard would study | a mage who hides his own life somewhere safe and keeps walking. old wizards love that sort of reading
B: so you admit it. merlin's in there with a new face | so lichas could be what's left of merlin
C: lichas is the apprentice who stayed behind to keep the door. my old guild's minstrel swore to it | i was always told lichas was a student who stayed to guard the barrow when merlin left
E: and merlin left his staff with albion. you dont leave your staff behind unless you mean to come back for it | either way, the staff stayed with us. a wizard doesnt leave his staff unless he plans to return
B: or he died holding it and someone picked it up | or he couldnt carry it any more
A: i still nod to the stones when i ride across salisbury. just in case | i nod at stonehenge every time i pass. if he's listening, i want to be on the list
?C: next run i'm knocking on the barrow walls. hollow walls have stories | i'll tap the walls of the barrow of merlin next time. something down there sounds hollow

@topic lore_inconnu
@levels 1-50
@scene alb_lore_trust_the_inconnu
@role B class=Necromancer
@role C type=NoLifer levels=25-50
@role D type=* levels=5-25
@role E type=Explorer,Daredevil
A: {q} honest question. do we actually trust the inconnu | do we trust the inconnu, or are we just glad they point the same way
?D: inconnu are the pale ones with the big eyes, right? what's their story? | sorry, the inconnu are a race? where did they come from?
C: they came up from below when the isles opened. arawn, lord of the dead, sent them, because someone was stealing his dead | they're arawn's people. arawn rules the underworld, someone has been stealing his dead, so he sent the inconnu to help us
B: and before anyone sneers, my whole class answers to arawn. the necromancers of albion have served him since the realm began | careful. every necromancer in albion draws power from arawn. he was on our side long before the inconnu came up
C: our side. the arawnites in the black mountains pray to him too, and they ambush travellers on the road | arawn has worshippers in the black mountains. arawnite warriors, headhunters, shamanesses. trust them too?
B: every lord has bad followers. we have friars who drink | a god with ugly worshippers. sounds like half the churches in camelot
E: and there's an eye of arawn in snowdonia. if he's our friend why is he watching our frontier | snowdonia has a thing called the eye of arawn. friends dont leave eyes in your yard
B: the war comes over snowdonia. a wise ally keeps an eye where the enemy walks in | maybe he watches because thats where the enemy crosses. an ally watches the gate
D: is arawn from the old legends or did someone make him up? | is arawn a real legend?
C: old welsh tale. arawn, king of annwn, the otherworld. a prince called pwyll swapped places with him for a year and a day | welsh legend. arawn ruled annwn, the land of the dead, and traded faces with a mortal prince, pwyll, for a year
B: and pwyll kept faith the whole year, never touched arawn's queen. arawn made him a friend for life. he repays loyalty | pwyll kept every promise for a year and arawn never forgot it. a lord who rewards honesty
C: the same tales have his hounds, white with red ears, running down souls. cwn annwn. some of those dogs are loose in our world | and his hounds, cwn annwn, white with red ears, hunting the dead. you can meet them out there. lovely friends
~ 15
E: and caer sidi is from the old poem too. arthur sailed into annwn with three shiploads of men and seven came back | the old poem says arthur raided the fortress of annwn. three ships went in, seven men came out
C: caer sidi sits on the border to his land, packed with imprisoned souls. if arawn is so mighty, why is his own doorstep rotting | his own back door is caer sidi and it's full of trapped souls. some kingdom
B: because someone is robbing him. that's the whole reason he sent help. you dont send help when you're winning | thats why he needs us. a lord who is losing asks for allies
C: and when he wins? a lord of the dead doesnt take thank-you notes. he'll send a bill and i can guess the coin | win his war and then what. lords of the dead get paid in souls
E: i've never once heard an inconnu say thank you. or why. they only ever tell you when | inconnu tell you when to charge and never why
D: the inconnu in my guild is the nicest person in it, for what it's worth | one of my guildmates is inconnu and she's always the first to help
B: there you go. the newest voice in the realm with the best argument | see. the newbie has more sense than the veterans
C: and a friendly face is exactly how a patient lord of the dead would buy trust | if i were the lord of the dead, i'd send the nice ones first
A: so we trust them and count our fingers after | trust them, ride with them, keep one eye open
?E: if an inconnu reads this channel we're all in trouble. they hear everything | careful, the inconnu hear more than they let on

@topic lore_green_knight
@levels 1-50
@scene alb_lore_green_knight_game
@role B type=Daredevil
@role C type=NoLifer levels=30-50
@role D type=* levels=5-25
@role E type=Explorer
A: {q} what is the green knight actually doing on the sauvage road | does anyone know why the green knight just stands in forest sauvage
?D: there's a green knight? like from the story? | the green knight from the old poem? he's here?
E: out in forest sauvage, not far from castle sauvage. a tall knight in green plate with a green blade, standing there, bothering nobody | in the frontier, sauvage. tall, green plate, green sword. he doesnt aggro, he just stands and watches the road
B: standing there daring someone to swing first. i respect it | waiting for someone brave enough to start it. one day that's me
C: he cons purple to every one of us. a patch made him as tough as midgard's glacier giant. that's not a dare, it's a warning | they toughened him in a patch to glacier giant strength. he's not a dare, he's a wall
D: what happens in the story he's from? | whats the old story?
E: a knight in green rides into arthur's hall at the yule feast and offers a game: strike me once, and in a year and a day i strike you back | a stranger all in green rides into the yule feast. one free blow, he says, and you take mine back in a year and a day
E: young gawain takes his head off. the knight picks it up by the hair and the head tells him: come find me at the green chapel | gawain cuts his head clean off. the knight lifts it and the head says see you next year
B: so the first swing is free. you all heard it | free first hit. sounds like a deal to me
C: the free swing is the trap. the whole point of the tale is that he doesnt die, and you owe him | the catch is in the tale. he doesnt die and you've just signed for his return blow
E: and gawain only lived because he came back honest. two feints and a nick on the neck | gawain lived because he kept his word. two feints, a nick on the neck, and a lesson
~ 15
C: i watched three groups go at him on a quiet frontier night once. death spam for a full minute, then he went back to standing | three groups tried him while i was passing once. the death spam scrolled for ages. then the road was quiet again
B: three groups is a warm-up. bring a proper raid and he falls | three groups is not a raid. bring forty and see
C: and get what? no keep, no relic, no realm points. you'd be killing a neighbour for a bad story | for what? he guards nothing. a neighbour who never bothered anyone
D: so he's not an enemy? | so is he on our side or not?
E: neither. look at who lives around him: fellwoods, walking trees, the gold oaken fellwood, a fallen druid seer | neutral. look at his neighbours. fellwoods, the gold oaken fellwood, a fallen druid seer. he belongs to the forest
E: i think he's the old green man of the woods, the face with leaves for a beard, in armour. the fellwoods are his court | the green man carved in old church roofs, put in plate. thats my guess. sauvage is his and the fellwoods are his household
C: the poem never made him a tree. he was a lord, bertilak, under a witch's spell, sent to test arthur's court | in the old poem he's a lord under an enchantment, sent by a witch to scare the round table. not a tree king
B: spell or not, the head comes off. i've read the story | fine, a lord under a spell. still has a head i can take
C: and the return blow comes a year and a day later. while you're shopping in cotswold | then a year on, he finds you. probably while you're at the bank in camelot
?D: someone said a fellwood quest out there gives a grail shaped charm. is that a joke? | i heard there's a grail shaped charm of fellwood out there. is that real?
E: real. somebody in that forest has read the old tales and laughs at them | real enough. the grail shaped charm of fellwood. that forest has a sense of humour
A: so the advice is nod politely and keep riding | right. nod, keep riding, owe nobody a blow
B: i'll nod. for now. one day i'm bringing a raid and a bard to write it down | i'll nod this time. but one day it's a raid and a minstrel taking notes
?C: tell the bard to write the second verse too. the one where he picks his head up | make sure the song covers what happens a year later

@topic lore_afanc
@levels 1-50
@scene alb_lore_afanc_drop
@role B type=Crafter
@role C type=Leveler levels=30-50
@role D type=* levels=5-25
@role E type=Explorer
A: {q} what does the legendary afanc actually drop. my guild says three different things | is the legendary afanc worth killing or is it a story with teeth
?D: whats an afanc? | afanc? never heard of it
E: a lake monster out of the welsh tales. ours lives in llyn barfog, with its hatchlings swimming all over the shallows | llyn barfog's lake beast. part beaver, part dragon, depends who tells it. the afanc hatchlings are all around the water
B: an old smith in ludlow swears it drops a hide that sheds water like oil. best leather in the realm, if you can get it | a crafter i trust swore it drops a scale that never rusts. hammer it into a shield and it shrugs off rain forever
C: i heard it drops a wet sack and a long walk back to the stable | i heard nothing drops but lake weed and repair bills
B: then why is someone in camelot selling "afanc hide" for a fortune. you dont fake what doesnt exist | someone in camelot sells afanc scale. nobody forges a thing that was never real
C: people sell fakes of things that never existed all the time. i once bought a map to avalon | people fake unicorn horns too. by that logic unicorns are camping lyonesse
D: is it a raid or can a group do it? | how strong is it? can a group take it?
C: it's big. the zone story says the beasts of llyn barfog gained epic status, and the afanc is the worst of them | big. the zone lore says the monsters of that lake grew to epic size, and the legendary afanc tops them all
E: and it's not alone up there. the king of the barfog hills, teazanodwc, mouth, fester. the whole lake is a nest of named things | the hills around it have names of their own. king of the barfog hills, teazanodwc, mouth and fester. busy neighbourhood
E: in the old tale arthur's horse dragged an afanc out of that very lake on a chain. the hoof print is still in the stone, carn march arthur | the legend says arthur chained an afanc and his horse hauled it out of the lake. the hoofprint is pressed in a stone up the hill
C: so which is it, the one arthur pulled out or another one | if arthur pulled it out, what's this one doing back in there
E: its grandchild, maybe. look at all the hatchlings. it's a family business | a descendant. or the same beast, walked back in. legends never stay out of lakes
~ 15
B: barfog is old welsh for bearded, you know. llyn barfog, the bearded lake | the name means bearded. the bearded lake
D: a lake with a beard? why? | why would a lake be bearded?
E: there's a sir dillus up there. in the old tale dillus the bearded had his beard plucked out with wooden tweezers to make a hound's leash | sir dillus walks those hills. in the old story dillus the bearded lost his beard hair by hair to make a hunting leash
C: thats a horrible way to make a leash. even crafters have standards | that is the worst crafting recipe i have ever heard
B: speak for yourself. a leash from a beard, a shield from an afanc. i'd craft with either if the quality was right | crafters do have standards. thats why i want that hide
C: real talk, i've never met anyone with that famous hide in their bags. coin, some loot, a story | honest answer: nobody i know ever came back with the scale or the hide. coin and a story
B: nobody you know. the ones who have it dont shout it across camelot | the people who have it dont advertise
A: so no proof of the drop and plenty of history. sounds like albion | no loot proof, lots of legend. normal albion
E: i'm riding up there anyway to see the hoof print stone. legends are worth more than leather | i'll go look at the stone with the hoof print. bring back nothing but a better story
?B: if you see a scale on the shore, it's mine. i called it | any scale you find on the beach, mail it to me

@topic lore_relics
@levels 1-50
@scene alb_lore_scabbard_not_blade
@role B type=NoLifer levels=30-50
@role C type=Explorer
@role D type=* levels=5-25
@role E class=Armsman,Paladin,Mercenary,Reaver
A: {q} why is the scabbard our relic and not excalibur itself | how did albion end up with a scabbard for a relic instead of the sword
?D: what are relics? i keep hearing about them in the frontier | relics? are those the things the keeps fight over?
B: two per realm, one for strength and one for power. ours are excalibur's scabbard and merlin's staff. hold theirs and your realm hits harder | a strength relic and a power relic each. ours are the scabbard and merlin's staff. steal the enemy's and the whole realm gets a bonus
E: the scabbard is the strength one. every fighter in albion swings harder because of a sheath. think about that | our melee relic is a sword holder. my swings hit harder because albion keeps a sheath safe
C: because the scabbard was always the real treasure. in the old tale merlin asked arthur which he valued more, blade or sheath | merlin asked arthur once which he liked better, the sword or the scabbard. arthur picked the sword. wrong answer
C: whoever wore the scabbard couldnt bleed. worth ten swords, merlin said. the sword just cut | the old tale says the sheath kept its wearer from losing blood. merlin said it was worth ten of the blade
B: and arthur lost it. his half sister stole it and threw it in a lake, and without it he bled out at camlann | arthur lost it, didnt he. his sister stole it and drowned it. without it he bled at camlann
D: so where's the sword then? | so what happened to excalibur?
C: back in the water. bedivere threw it into the lake when arthur was dying, and a hand came up and took it under | the dying king made bedivere throw it in the lake. a hand rose out of the water, caught it and sank
E: after he hid it in the reeds twice and lied about it. bedivere, worst errand runner in albion | took him three tries. hid it in the grass twice and lied. sounds like my guild with quest items
B: so the lady has it. lady nimue walks avalon marsh after dark. go ask her for it | so the lady of the lake has it. nimue walks the marsh at night. ask her nicely
C: con her once. nobody is prying anything out of those hands | nobody is taking a sword from her. con her and you'll see why
~ 15
B: the relic raids are the better story anyway. lose the scabbard to midgard and every tavern sings about it for a month | the real excalibur fight is relic raids. lose the scabbard and you hear about it for weeks
E: the first time we lost it, faction chat was a funeral. nobody logged off | i still remember the first relic we lost. the whole realm was in the frontier within the hour
D: can they really just take it? | does that happen a lot?
B: break the relic keep, carry it home, survive the ride. it has happened both ways. that's what the frontier is for | it happens. break the keep, walk it to your own relic keep, live through the ride. rvr at its best
C: and when the relics first came, the word was more would follow. none ever did | the heralds once promised more relics. they never came
C: i think the sword is the one they held back. the day the lady lets go of excalibur, the realms go to war over it | the missing relic is the blade, i'm sure of it. one day the lady gives it up and all three realms come for it
B: and the hibs would swear the lady of the lake was one of their elves all along | if they add the sword the hibs will claim the lady was a lurikeen
E: the day excalibur is a relic, the mids will camp the marsh till the leeches eat them | if the sword ever shows up the mids will be camping avalon marsh in shifts
A: until then, guard the scabbard and bow if you pass the lady | for now, keep the scabbard home and bow to the lady
?C: i do bow, every time she passes. she nodded back once, i swear | i bow every time. she looked at me once. i'm counting it

@topic lore_oldest
@levels 1-50
@scene alb_lore_oldest_in_albion
@role B type=NoLifer levels=30-50
@role C type=Explorer
@role D type=* levels=5-25
@role E type=Socialite
A: {q} what's the oldest thing in albion, honestly | oldest legend in albion. go
B: golestandt. in uther's day a roman lord and his household vanished in dartmoor, and she was already home | the dragon. a roman lord vanished on the moor back in uther's time. she was there first
C: the giants were here before her. in the old chronicles albion was giant country until brutus of troy landed and drove them out | older than dragons: giants. the old books say albion belonged to giants before brutus came from troy
?D: brutus of troy? like the troy with the wooden horse? | who was brutus?
C: a trojan prince who sailed west after the war, the old books say. he named britain after himself and gave cornwall to his friend corineus | grandson of aeneas, so the story goes. landed here, fought the giants, gave the island his name. his man corineus got cornwall
C: corineus wrestled the giant gogmagog and threw him off a cliff into the sea. and what still walks cornwall? cornish giants | corineus threw the chief giant off a cornish cliff. and today cornwall is full of cornish giants
E: so the cornish giants are sore losers waiting for a rematch | cornish giants holding a grudge for three thousand years. respect
B: and the boulders in dartmoor are granite giants, and the stonecrush ogres chew on them, and all of them keep clear of her | the granite giants are dartmoor itself. the stonecrush ogres eat them. everyone stays out of her way
C: dragons outlive kings, fine, but stone outlives dragons | i still say rock is older than any dragon
D: what about stonehenge? isnt that super old? | stonehenge has to be old, right?
E: the stones were standing long before anyone named a barrow after merlin. he just moved in | stonehenge was ancient already when they buried kings under it
C: the old name for it is the giants' dance. giants carried those stones first, merlin only moved them. giants again | in the old tale merlin brought the giants' dance from over the sea. giants raised it first
B: is there any question where your answer isnt giants | you'd make the giants the answer to everything
~ 15
E: keltoi fogou then. the carvings aren't roman, aren't anything with a name, and the keltoi priests are still chanting in there | the fogou. a holy cave before rome had a name, and the keltoi are still down there chanting
B: chanting, and adding on every single pull | chanting and bringing friends to every fight
C: then lyonesse. cailleach uragaig walks there, and the cailleach of the old tales is the hag of winter who dropped the hills from her apron | lyonesse has cailleach uragaig. in the old stories the cailleach built the mountains out of stones she spilled from her apron
E: so the oldest thing in albion is somebody's grandmother. sounds right | the oldest thing is an old woman with an apron. every village knows that
D: someone told me about a ghost bell in the black mountains too | i heard there's an abbey bell in the black mountains that rings by itself
B: the vetusta abbey bell. vetusta is latin for ancient. even the name is cheating at this game | vetusta abbey. the name means old in latin. whoever named it knew what they were doing
C: and llyn barfog had its afanc before arthur was born. he only got the credit for pulling one out | the lake had its monster long before arthur dragged one ashore
A: so no winner. i'm writing it down as all of them | no answer as usual. albion is old all the way down
B: put golestandt at the top. she might be reading | write her first. she's a dragon, she can read chat
?C: i'm off to the cornish cliffs to look for where gogmagog went over. somebody has to | i'm riding to cornwall to find the cliff corineus used. if there's a giant's heel mark at the edge, i'll tell you

# ---- Tale scenes: one bot tells an Albion legend, others interrupt ----
@topic lore_tale_arward
@levels 1-50
@scene alb_tale_arwards_knights
@role B type=* levels=5-25
@role C type=NoLifer,Leveler
@role D type=Socialite,Daredevil
A: {q} want to hear how albion first learned the name golestandt? | here's the old dartmoor story. how we learned the dragon's name
?B: go on, all i know is dont go south in dartmoor | sure, i only know her as the big con at the bottom of dartmoor
A: back when uther was king, a roman lord kept a house out on the moor. one season the letters stopped. household, servants, horses, gone | in uther's day a roman lord lived on dartmoor with his whole household. one year they simply stopped answering
A: uther sent sir arward with thirty knights to find out why, and a scribe to write it all down | so the king sent sir arward and thirty knights. and a scribe, because someone always has to keep the record
C: thirty knights for one missing roman. that lord must have owed the crown a fortune | uther sent thirty knights for one roman? he must have owed uther money
A: the journal tells of boulders that stood up and walked, the granite giants, and ogres that ate the rock, the stonecrush | the scribe wrote of stones that got up and walked, and of the stonecrush ogres chewing granite giants like bread
B: wait, the stonecrush ogres in dartmoor are from that story? | the stonecrush? those are still out on the moor
A: still there, still chewing. the knights rode south past all of it. the entries get shorter. then nothing | still hungry too. arward pushed south, deeper into the moor. the pages thin out. then they stop
~ 12
A: months later a sentry at prydwen keep saw something fall out of a clear sky. a book, half burnt, still smoking | months on, a guard at prydwen watched a scorched book drop out of an empty sky and land at his feet
C: from dartmoor to prydwen through the air. thats some throw | thats a long way for a book to fly. something threw it
A: most pages were ash. one word could still be read, written large: golestandt | the pages were cinders. one word survived the fire. golestandt
D: and the thirty knights? and arward? | so what happened to arward and his men?
A: never found. not a horse, not a helm. the moor kept them all, and the journal kept her name | no knight came back. dartmoor kept the men and gave us only the name
?C: i'll believe the flying book when one lands on my head at prydwen | if a burnt book ever hits me at prydwen keep i'll know who sent it

@topic lore_tale_cardova
@levels 1-50
@scene alb_tale_nameless_emperor
@role B type=NoLifer
@role C type=* levels=5-25
@role D type=Explorer
A: {q} my old guild's minstrel had a story about the emperor under cardova. want it? | here's the cardova story the way an old minstrel told it to me. the emperor nobody can name
A: it starts in britain. a general here was hailed emperor by his own legions and marched them over the sea, like macsen in the welsh tale | a commander in britain was named emperor by his troops, like macsen wledig in the old welsh story, and took the legions to win rome
?C: who's macsen? | macsen?
A: an emperor out of britain in the welsh tale. he dreamed of a maiden in a far land, crossed the sea to find her and stayed. ours is darker | in the welsh story macsen dreams of a girl across the sea, goes to find her and never comes home. a love story. this one isnt
A: ours lost. killed far away. but his legion would not leave him in foreign dirt. they carried him home across the sea by night | our emperor fell over the sea, the minstrel said, and his legion carried his body all the way back to cornwall
B: carrying a dead emperor across a whole continent. in armour. with the smell | across an entire continent with a corpse. loyal, i'll give them that
A: they cut cardova out of the cornish rock and laid him deep. then rome, which hated him, ordered his name struck from every stone | they dug the crypt halls and sealed him in. and rome ordered his name chiselled off everything, the way it did with emperors it hated
A: so his own priests scraped it off the tomb first. a man with no name cant be cursed by his enemies, they said | his priests scratched the name away before rome could. no name, no curse
D: clever. and terrible. very roman | thats clever and sad at once
A: and the legion swore to stand watch until someone speaks his name in the crypt. nobody knows it. so the watch never ends | the legion's oath: guard him until his name is said aloud in those halls. nobody can say it, so they still stand
B: and emperor seleucus? | then who's seleucus?
A: the general who took the empty chair, she said. the forgotten emperor is the real one, and seleucus sits in his seat | a pretender, the minstrel said. the forgotten one is our man and seleucus is wearing his crown
?B: lovely tale. tell it to a centurion and see if he lets you pass | nice story. i'll believe it when a praetorian stands down
?D: next time i'm down there i'm shouting every roman name i know at the forgotten emperor | i'm going to try every latin name i can think of in front of him

@topic lore_tale_green_knight
@levels 1-50
@scene alb_tale_green_knight_road
@role B type=* levels=5-25
@role C type=NoLifer
@role D type=Daredevil,Socialite
A: {q} want to know why the green knight stands in sauvage? an old armsman told me the story | here's the story i heard about the green knight and the sauvage road
A: you know the old game, a blow for a blow, the return in a year and a day. the armsman said more than gawain played it | the beheading game. the armsman said gawain wasnt the only one who took a swing. a whole bench of knights did
?B: more than gawain? who else? | wait, others swung at him too?
A: every proud knight at the table, after gawain came home with that nick on his neck. each one swung, each one booked a return | after gawain came back alive, every hothead at the round table wanted a go. each swing, another blow owed
A: then camlann came before the year was out. the table broken, the knights dead or scattered. nobody left to collect from | and before the year was up, camlann. table broken, knights dead or gone. a ledger full of blows and no one to pay
C: so he's a debt collector. albion's oldest debt collector | so he's standing there with a bill. very albion
A: so he took the road through sauvage, the one every albion knight rides to the frontier sooner or later, and he waits | he picked the road north that every knight of albion has to ride one day. and he stands there, waiting
B: but those knights are long dead | the round table's been dust for ages though
A: the armsman said the debt passes to whoever strikes him next. hit him and you inherit some dead knight's return blow | swing at him, the armsman said, and you take a dead knight's place in his book
D: that's why he never swings first. he's waiting for a volunteer | that explains the not attacking. he wants someone to sign
A: and those groups that tried him and died? the armsman said that wasnt a fight. that was him collecting | the groups he wiped? not combat, the armsman said. a receipt
?C: nice tale. i bet the fellwoods tell it differently | the fellwoods probably have their own version
?D: next time i'm through sauvage i'm keeping both hands on the reins | i'll nod to him next time and keep my hands off my sword

@topic lore_tale_lyonesse
@levels 1-50
@scene alb_tale_lyonesse_drowning
@role B type=* levels=5-25
@role C type=NoLifer,Leveler
@role D type=Explorer
A: {q} the camelot bards tell the drowning of lyonesse two ways. want the one-night version? | here's the lyonesse story, the way an old bard in camelot told it to me
?B: drowning? we level in lyonesse, it's dry land | wait, lyonesse isnt underwater. i've walked through it
A: patience. in the old tale lyonesse ran from cornwall out into the western sea. fields, a city, a hundred and forty churches | in the old tale lyonesse was a rich country past cornwall, out where the sea is now. farms, towns, a hundred and forty churches
A: in one night the sea rose and took all of it. one man got out, riding a white horse ahead of the waves to the cornish hills | then one night the sea came up and swallowed the lot. one rider escaped on a white horse, the water at his heels
C: one night, or a hundred years of the sea creeping in. depends which bard has had more ale | the other version is a century of slow flooding. less dramatic, more likely
A: one night. a hundred years doesnt get a ballad | one night makes the song. nobody sings about slow flooding
D: there's a white horse near west downs in salisbury. same horse? | heh, a white horse wanders around west downs. you think it's that one
A: the bard swore it was. still running, still afraid of water. i never checked | the bard said yes. still running from the sea after all these years
B: so what's the lyonesse we play in then? | then what is our lyonesse?
A: what the sea left, the bard said. the high ground. the drowned country lies past the coast, and the church bells still ring down there | the hilltops that stayed dry, the bard said. the rest is under the waves, bells and all
C: you hear bells anywhere if you want to. albion collects haunted bells, there's one at vetusta abbey too | bells under the sea. the vetusta abbey bell in the black mountains rings with nobody pulling it either
A: and who walks the dry part now? cailleach uragaig. a hag of the old world who watched her country go under | and cailleach uragaig keeps the dry part. an old witch who saw it all drown and stayed
?D: next time i'm in lyonesse i'm swimming out to listen for bells | i'm swimming out past the coast to listen. if i hear a bell, i'm turning around
?C: if you hear bells, swim faster. the sea doesnt give people back | take a healer. the sea keeps what it takes

@topic lore_tale_inconnu
@levels 1-50
@scene alb_tale_inconnu_came
@role B class=Necromancer
@role C type=NoLifer
@role D type=* levels=5-25
A: {q} how did the inconnu end up in albion anyway? i never got the story | anyone know how the inconnu came to albion? they just showed up with the isles
B: my trainer told it this way. arawn rules annwn, the land of the dead, and every soul in albion goes to him in the end | the way the necromancers tell it: every albion soul ends up in annwn, arawn's realm. that's the old bargain
B: then his dead started going missing, raised before they reached him. old roman legions marching again. souls locked in caer sidi | then somebody started stealing his dead. dead legions walking again, souls caged at caer sidi. arawn noticed
?D: stealing dead people? who? | who was stealing them?
C: the same witch who set the drakoran on avalon. nobody says her name in camelot | the one who sent the drakoran into avalon. you know who. we dont say it
B: so arawn opened the deep doors and sent his pale servants up. the inconnu. eyes made for the dark, faces that never saw sun | arawn answered by sending his own people up from below. the inconnu. pale as candles, eyes made for the dark
B: they came up on the isles where the war was, and they chose to stand with albion, because the thief is our enemy too | they surfaced right where the drakoran were winning, and they picked our side
C: picked our side. or were told to | told to, more like. arawn sends, they go
D: so are the inconnu undead? | are they dead too?
B: no. living, but from close to his kingdom. they can tell arawn's dead from the stolen ones the way we tell rain from snow | living. but they know the dead the way we know weather. which belong to arawn and which were taken
C: a whole people who can count souls. handy, and a little creepy | that's the part that worries me
B: in the old welsh tale arawn gave lifelong friendship to a prince who kept his word for a year. albion has kept its word. so far | there's an old story where arawn rewards a mortal for one honest year. keep faith with arawn and he keeps faith with you
?A: so they're here as long as the thief is | so they stay until the thief is caught
?C: and when she's caught, someone will have to pay arawn's bill | and after that, we'll see what arawn wants for his help

